      "problem": "If $4{y}^{2}+12y+9=0,$ then $y=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}+12y+9 & =0 \\\\ (2y)^{2}+2\\cdot 2y\\cdot 3+{3}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2y+3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(2y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |2y+3| & =0 \\\\ 2y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
